CAT = "mlsBbProd"; SEG = "BPR"
Q = [
("Under FDA rules, cryoprecipitated AHF must average at least how much factor VIII per container?",
 "80 units", ["40 units", "150 units", "250 units"],
 "21 CFR 640.54 requires a procedure that yields an average of no less than 80 units of antihemophilic factor per final container. 150 mg is the familiar fibrinogen figure, not the factor VIII one."),
("Fresh frozen plasma must be placed in a freezer within what time of collection?",
 "8 hours", ["4 hours", "24 hours", "72 hours"],
 "21 CFR 640.34 sets 8 hours (or the collection system's stated time) for FFP. Plasma frozen within 24 hours after phlebotomy is a separate product, PF24."),
("Red cells irradiated on day 10 of a 42-day shelf life now expire:",
 "28 days after irradiation", ["42 days after collection", "14 days after irradiation", "24 hours after irradiation"],
 "21 CFR 610.53 gives irradiated red cells 28 days from irradiation or the original dating, whichever is shorter. Day 10 plus 28 days is day 38, before the original day 42."),
("Red cells deglycerolized in a closed system with additive solution added expire after:",
 "14 days", ["24 hours", "5 days", "42 days"],
 "21 CFR 610.53 allows 14 days for closed-system deglycerolized red cells with additive. Cells deglycerolized in an open system expire 24 hours after the bag is entered."),
("Glycerolized red cells kept at -65 C or colder have a dating period of:",
 "10 years from collection", ["1 year after freezing date", "5 years from collection", "42 days from collection"],
 "21 CFR 610.53 dates frozen red cells stored at -65 C or colder 10 years from collection. One year is the dating for FFP and cryoprecipitate at -18 C."),
("Liquid Plasma stored at 1 to 6 C expires:",
 "5 days after whole blood expiry", ["5 days after it is separated", "1 year from the collection date", "24 hours after the bag is entered"],
 "21 CFR 610.53 dates Liquid Plasma 5 days from the end of the whole blood dating period. It is never frozen, so it carries lower labile factor levels."),
("Compared with FFP, plasma frozen within 24 hours of phlebotomy has slightly less:",
 "Factor V and factor VIII", ["Fibrinogen and factor XIII", "Factors II, VII, IX and X", "Albumin and antithrombin"],
 "The labile factors V and VIII fall during the extra hours before freezing; the stable factors and fibrinogen are similar. Both products are used for the same indications."),
("Acute hypoxemia with bilateral lung infiltrates 2 hours after plasma, with normal blood pressure and a normal BNP, suggests:",
 "Transfusion-related acute lung injury", ["Transfusion-associated circulatory overload", "Allergic transfusion reaction", "Septic transfusion reaction"],
 "TRALI is noncardiogenic lung edema within 6 hours of transfusion, often from donor anti-HLA or anti-HNA. A normal BNP and no hypertension argue against overload."),
("Nine days after transfusion a patient's hemoglobin falls, the DAT is positive and a new anti-Jka is found. This is:",
 "Delayed hemolytic transfusion reaction", ["Acute hemolytic transfusion reaction", "Delayed serologic transfusion reaction", "Febrile nonhemolytic reaction"],
 "An anamnestic Kidd antibody that destroys the transfused cells days later is a delayed hemolytic reaction. A delayed serologic reaction has the new antibody but no hemolysis."),
("After a fatal transfusion reaction, the written report to FDA's CBER must be sent within:",
 "7 days of the death", ["24 hours of the death", "30 days of the death", "45 days of the death"],
 "21 CFR 606.170 requires notice to CBER as soon as possible and a written investigation report within 7 days, from the facility that did the compatibility testing."),
("Tingling around the mouth during rapid transfusion of many units is most likely caused by:",
 "Citrate binding ionized calcium", ["Potassium leaking from stored cells", "Cold units lowering body temperature", "Air entering the infusion line"],
 "Citrate anticoagulant chelates ionized calcium when given faster than the liver clears it, causing paresthesias. Hyperkalemia and hypothermia are other massive transfusion risks with different signs."),
("Why must granulocyte concentrates be ABO compatible with the recipient?",
 "They contain many red cells", ["They contain no donor plasma", "They are always leukoreduced", "They lack HLA antigens"],
 "Granulocyte concentrates are heavily contaminated with red cells, so they are crossmatched like red cells. They are irradiated, never leukoreduced, since the white cells are the product."),
("Prestorage leukoreduction lowers febrile nonhemolytic reactions mainly by reducing:",
 "Cytokines released during storage", ["Plasma proteins in the unit", "Red cell antigen exposure", "Bacterial growth in the stored bag"],
 "White cells release cytokines such as IL-1 and IL-6 during storage; removing them before storage prevents that buildup. Plasma proteins drive allergic, not febrile, reactions."),
("Platelets rise from 10 to 30 x 10^3/µL after 3.6 x 10^11 platelets in a patient with 1.8 m2 body surface area. What is the corrected count increment?",
 "10,000", ["5,556", "15,000", "36,000"],
 "CCI = increment x BSA / dose = 20,000 x 1.8 / 3.6 = 10,000. Using the post count instead of the increment gives 15,000; leaving out BSA gives 5,556."),
("For emergency release before typing, which red cells are usually issued to a young woman of unknown type?",
 "Group O, Rh negative", ["Group O, Rh positive", "Group AB, Rh negative", "Group A, Rh negative"],
 "Group O cells lack A and B antigens, and Rh-negative cells avoid anti-D formation in a woman who may later become pregnant. The ordering physician signs for the uncrossmatched units."),
("Therapeutic plasma exchange for TTP usually replaces the removed plasma with:",
 "Donor plasma", ["5% albumin", "0.9% saline", "Hydroxyethyl starch"],
 "Exchange with plasma removes the anti-ADAMTS13 antibody and replaces the missing ADAMTS13 enzyme. Albumin replacement supplies no enzyme."),
]
